    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1255">

                <pb facs="1243" n="1233" sameAs="frus:frus1949v09/pg1233"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/3–349: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Cabot</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-03-03T18:00:00+08:00">March 3, 1949—6 p.
                            m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 3—6:52 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>735. [To Nanking:] I have discussed with Mayor Wu proposed contribution some 300
                    million GY<anchor n="47" xml:id="d1255-f1"/> by Shanghai guilds to maintain
                    soldiery now quartered in city. I pointed out Consulate General and American
                    business were seriously concerned at possibility latter would be asked make
                    voluntary contribution to one side in Chinese Civil War. I added American
                    business would, of course, raise no question about legally enforceable
                    taxes.</p>
                <p>Mayor Wu pointed out funds for maintenance troops quartered this area are wholly
                    insufficient for their food, pay, etc., that many incidents showed soldiery were
                    getting out of hand and contribution was designed keep them satisfied. When I
                    expressed my sympathy with his objective but repeated my misgivings re voluntary
                    rather than <pb facs="1244" n="1234" xml:id="pg1234"/> legally enforceable
                    contributions, Mayor Wu claimed this contribution would be legally enforceable
                    under municipal] ordinance. Despite his assurances, I am frankly not satisfied
                    this contribution when promulgated will be legally enforceable and I am
                    convinced Mayor Wu is not really satisfied on this point either.</p>
                <p>Embassy may wish discuss this matter very informally in Nanking or wait until we
                    have full details re measure before taking any action.</p>
                <p>Sent Nanking 462, repeated Dept 735, OffEmb Canton 118.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cabot</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1255-f1" n="47" xml:id="d1255-f1-content">
